WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.835
Before Rosa’s application reaches the home office, Theo

00:00:03.835 --> 00:00:07.191
has already underwritten it in her kitchen.

00:00:07.191 --> 00:00:12.943
Field underwriting means the producer’s screening of the applicant: observing her health

00:00:12.943 --> 00:00:18.696
and occupation, asking every question on the form, recording answers exactly as

00:00:18.696 --> 00:00:24.448
given and noting anything the insurer should know in the producer’s report.

00:00:24.448 --> 00:00:30.680
Good field underwriting saves the insurer from issuing policies it would have declined

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:36.433
and saves applicants from policies that would be rescinded later for misstatements.

00:00:36.433 --> 00:00:40.268
It also protects the pool from adverse selection.

00:00:40.268 --> 00:00:46.020
The producer who skips this role invites claim disputes and license complaints.

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:48.826
The application must be complete and accurate.

00:00:48.826 --> 00:00:54.039
Theo reads each question aloud and writes Rosa’s answer; he never guesses or

00:00:54.039 --> 00:00:59.251
leaves a blank, because the insurer may treat an unanswered question as waived.

00:00:59.251 --> 00:01:02.859
If an answer is corrected, Rosa initials the change.

00:01:02.859 --> 00:01:07.670
She signs the application, and so does Theo as the witnessing producer.

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:12.080
If Rosa pays the first premium now, Theo gives a conditional

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:16.491
receipt, which makes coverage effective as of the application or exam

00:01:16.491 --> 00:01:20.500
date if the insurer later approves her as applied for.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:24.910
If she does not pay now, no coverage exists until delivery.

00:01:24.910 --> 00:01:29.447
When the policy arrives, Theo delivers it promptly and in person when he can.

00:01:29.447 --> 00:01:33.011
If the first premium was not paid with the application, he

00:01:33.011 --> 00:01:36.576
collects it and obtains a statement of continued good health, in

00:01:36.576 --> 00:01:40.141
which Rosa confirms her health has not changed since she applied.

00:01:40.141 --> 00:01:43.705
The policy is not in force until that premium is paid.

00:01:43.705 --> 00:01:48.242
Theo reviews the policy against the application, explains the benefits, exclusions, renewability and the

00:01:48.242 --> 00:01:52.779
free-look period, and delivers any required outline of coverage and the guaranty corporation notice.

00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:55.372
He has Rosa sign a delivery receipt, which

00:01:55.372 --> 00:01:57.640
fixes the date the free-look period begins.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:03.937
Even careful producers make mistakes: a benefit amount misquoted, a replacement handled badly,

00:02:03.937 --> 00:02:10.235
an application mailed late so a client was uninsured when a claim arose.

00:02:10.235 --> 00:02:16.532
Errors and omissions insurance is professional liability coverage that pays defense costs and

00:02:16.532 --> 00:02:22.345
damages when a client sues over the producer’s negligent act or omission.

00:02:22.345 --> 00:02:28.643
It does not cover fraud, intentional misconduct or fines imposed by the regulator.

00:02:28.643 --> 00:02:33.003
Agencies carry it for the firm and its producers.

00:02:33.003 --> 00:02:38.331
The best E and O protection is process: documented recommendations, signed

00:02:38.331 --> 00:02:43.660
applications, delivery receipts and notes of what the client was told.

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:48.575
Dennis, a client with an individual medical plan, turns 65 in March.

00:02:48.575 --> 00:02:52.671
Theo has a responsibility to tell him what that means.

00:02:52.671 --> 00:02:56.767
Medicare eligibility changes how his coverage coordinates, opens a seven-month

00:02:56.767 --> 00:03:00.863
initial enrollment window around his birthday, and starts the six-month

00:03:00.863 --> 00:03:04.959
Medicare supplement open enrollment when he enrolls in Part B.

00:03:04.959 --> 00:03:10.693
Group plans and Maryland continuation coverage end or change when Medicare entitlement begins, and

00:03:10.693 --> 00:03:16.018
late enrollment in Part B or Part D can bring permanent premium penalties.

00:03:16.018 --> 00:03:20.114
Notifying clients of Medicare eligibility in time is part of

00:03:20.114 --> 00:03:24.210
serving them, and insurers include similar notices in group certificates.

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:26.108
Here is the sequence.

00:03:26.108 --> 00:03:30.378
Theo screens Rosa honestly and records her answers exactly.

00:03:30.378 --> 00:03:35.596
She signs; he gives a conditional receipt if she pays now.

00:03:35.596 --> 00:03:39.866
The home office underwrites using the sources from Lesson

00:03:39.866 --> 00:03:43.662
12 and issues the policy, perhaps with changes.

00:03:43.662 --> 00:03:50.304
Theo delivers it, collects any premium due, gets a statement of continued good health

00:03:50.304 --> 00:03:56.946
if needed, explains the contract and starts the free-look clock with a signed receipt.

00:03:56.946 --> 00:04:00.741
He keeps records in case a question arises

00:04:00.741 --> 00:04:04.062
later, and he calendars Dennis’s 65th birthday.

00:04:04.062 --> 00:04:10.230
Each step has a purpose: an enforceable, understood contract and a protected client.
